course: dictionary
lesson: 9674
title: Formal words: succumb
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words sound grown-up because they belong to formal writing, such as news reports, history books and exam papers. Succumb is one of these words.
explain: To succumb means to stop fighting something and give in to it. In stories a hero «succumbs to temptation», so he gives in to something he knows he should resist.
explain: You can also succumb to sleep or tiredness. It means you are overcome by it and cannot keep going. The word after succumb is usually «to».
explain: The news may say someone «succumbed to his injuries». This is a gentle, formal way of saying he died from them. Use the context to check: what is the sentence really telling you?
fact: Succumb is a verb: succumb, succumbs, succumbed, succumbing.
fact: Succumb is usually followed by «to», as in «succumb to temptation».
fact: «Succumb» means to give in to something, or to be overcome by it, such as temptation, pressure or sleep.
fact: In official reports and the news, «succumbed to his injuries» means he died from his injuries.
fact: Succumb is a formal word. In everyday talk you could say give in, or, for an illness or injury, died.
exercise: The hero «succumbs to temptation» and eats the forbidden cake. What does succumb mean here? | *Gives in to something he should resist | Sings a happy song | Counts all the cakes | Runs away very fast
exercise: The report says the rescuer «succumbed to his injuries». What does this mean? | He got better quickly | He went to sleep for a long time | *He died from his injuries | He won the race
exercise: Which word is the verb in «She succumbed to sleep on the sofa»? | sleep | *succumbed | sofa | she
test: Which small word usually comes after succumb? | with | *to | from | about
test: The news says «he succumbed to his injuries». Which simpler word is the best swap? | slept | danced | smiled | *died
exam: Which sentence uses succumb correctly? | The runner succumbed the trophy and cheered. | The dog succumbed his bone to the rain. | *She succumbed to the pressure and gave up her plan. | The boy succumb a sandwich very quickly.
